# ── the native shells ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# android/ and ios/ ARE committed, deliberately. Capacitor generates them once,
# and then they stop being generated output and become source: the permission
# list, the purpose strings, the icons and the signing config are all decisions,
# and losing them to a regenerate is how a build starts crashing the first time
# somebody taps the shutter.
#
# What is NOT committed is the copy of mobile/ that `cap sync` stamps into each
# project. That is genuinely build output — every asset duplicated, and stale
# the moment mobile/ changes. `npx cap sync` puts it back.
android/app/src/main/assets/public/
ios/App/App/public/
android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.config.json
android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.plugins.json
ios/App/App/capacitor.config.json
ios/App/App/config.xml

# Gradle and Xcode working files
android/.gradle/
android/build/
android/app/build/
android/local.properties
android/capacitor-cordova-android-plugins/
ios/App/Pods/
ios/App/build/
ios/App/App.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
ios/App/App.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
*.xcuserstate

node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
package-lock.json

# Calibration renders — 19 MB of PNGs regenerated by docs/uc-calibrate.py
# whenever a box or a crop changes. The inputs are in the repository; these
# are the picture of them, and a picture that is stale is worse than none.
docs/uc-calib/
